<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Two Peters</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1356">1356</a>–<a href="/years/1369">1369</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Two Peters was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter I of Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a> from 1356. Castilian armies repeatedly invaded Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, and the conflict merged with the Castilian civil war, in which Aragon backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-castile">Henry of Trastámara</a>. It ended with Peter I's death at Montiel in 1369, though a final peace came only in 1375.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>At the start of the second half of the fourteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was torn by the civil war between its king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter of Castile</a>, and his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-castile">Henry of Trastámara</a>. Peter of Castile sought the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elche">Elche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orihuela">Orihuela</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a> wished to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> against Castile and its ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. A naval incident raised tension: Catalan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> under Mossèn Francesc de Perellós, sailing with letters of marque from the King of Aragon, seized two Genoese ships at Sanlúcar de Barrameda, and Peter of Castile chased him as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tavira">Tavira</a> without catching him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter of Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a>, who also supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-castile">Henry of Trastámara</a>, and the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and Portugal were drawn in as well. The war can thus be seen as an extension of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> and also of the First Castilian Civil War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The fighting lay mostly along the border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarazona">Tarazona</a> on 9 March 1357, and a truce followed on 8 May. After Castilian gains early in 1361 the peace of Terrer of 18 May 1361 returned all conquered places to their lords. In June 1362 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter of Castile</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre">Charles II of Navarre</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>, then invaded Aragon without a declaration of war while Peter IV was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a> without troops; he failed, however, to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a>.</p><p>In 1363 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarazona">Tarazona</a> again, then Cariñena, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a>, Segorbe and Morvedre, and pressed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, Elda and Gandia; the peace of Morvedre arranged by the papal nuncio Jean de la Grange on 2 July 1363 was never ratified. With Moorish allies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> the Castilians ravaged southern Valencia and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orihuela">Orihuela</a> without success in 1364. From 1366 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter of Castile</a> was fighting for his own throne against Henry, who had the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-du-guesclin">Bertrand du Guesclin</a> and his free companies, until Henry killed him at Montiel in 1369.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> recovered districts that had passed to Aragon, among them the lordship of Molina, and Peter IV's daughter Eleanor married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile">John I of Castile</a>. Though Aragon gained the upper hand, the war, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, drought and locusts, ruined its economy and reduced its population, and the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarazona">Tarazona</a> was destroyed. The war is also believed to have built up the administrative and military forces that led to the union of Castile and Aragon in the next century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Two Pedros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Peter IV fought the long <b>War of the Two Peters</b> against Castile from 1356 to 1369, in which Aragon suffered repeated invasions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>War of the Two Peters</b>, eight hundred Aragonese horsemen, many of them Castilian exiles under Henry of Trastámara, routed a Castilian frontier force near Ágreda.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-22">22 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
